WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.947
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.947 --> 00:00:02.097
Friday the sixth.

00:00:02.097 --> 00:00:06.292
A wet start, a short workday, and a night out on the calendar.

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:08.796
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:08.796 --> 00:00:13.396
Once a private link leaks, what's the only real way to take it back?

00:00:13.396 --> 00:00:14.750
Answer's at the end.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.321
Weather's the main event this morning.

00:00:17.321 --> 00:00:27.266
Steady rain from about seven, heaviest through the morning ride, then it eases into drizzle after lunch and should be done by around eight tonight.

00:00:27.266 --> 00:00:29.905
Mild, mid-sixties, and barely any wind.

00:00:29.905 --> 00:00:30.852
Okay, the day.

00:00:30.852 --> 00:00:33.897
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets.

00:00:33.897 --> 00:00:36.874
Then Godinger, only a half day, nine to one.

00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:39.039
After that the afternoon's open.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:45.196
Dinner's on there at quarter past five, and then the Buena Vista Social Club show at seven.

00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:49.594
By the time you're heading out, the rain should be close to done.

00:00:49.594 --> 00:00:50.541
Alright, news.

00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:51.488
One about A.I.

00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:52.368
and security.

00:00:52.368 --> 00:01:01.096
OpenAI says an automated system of theirs went hunting for holes in Linux, the software that runs most of the internet's servers.

00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:10.636
It flagged over two thousand possible bugs, and for nine hundred and forty-eight of them it built a working attack that takes over a machine.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:17.063
The whole run cost about one point two million dollars, roughly thirteen hundred bucks per bug.

00:01:17.063 --> 00:01:22.747
People review the fixes before they go out, and so far only about sixty are patched.

00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:25.521
So the finding is getting cheap and fast.

00:01:25.521 --> 00:01:27.889
The fixing is still slow and human.

00:01:27.889 --> 00:01:30.054
And a small one that says a lot.

00:01:30.054 --> 00:01:33.910
Apple is cutting how much its own staff can spend on A.I.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:34.925
tools each day.

00:01:34.925 --> 00:01:41.826
Some engineers go from three hundred dollars a day down to two hundred, and there's talk of a hundred.

00:01:41.826 --> 00:01:45.412
Some non-engineering teams could drop to twenty-five.

00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:50.013
Apple's pushing people toward cheaper models that run on the device.

00:01:50.013 --> 00:01:52.516
Even at a company that size, the A.I.

00:01:52.516 --> 00:01:54.546
bill is real enough to ration.

00:01:54.546 --> 00:02:03.206
Further away, the World Health Organization approved trials for two new vaccines against the strain of Ebola spreading in Congo.

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:13.694
It's the biggest outbreak the country has ever had, more than fifty-three hundred deaths since May, and this strain kills about half the people it infects.

00:02:13.694 --> 00:02:16.671
Early results are hoped for early next year.

00:02:16.671 --> 00:02:19.986
Okay, here's one for next Friday, the thirteenth.

00:02:19.986 --> 00:02:27.225
House of Yes, on Wyckoff, has an early show called Dirty Circus, a variety night that starts at six-thirty.

00:02:27.225 --> 00:02:29.458
The early start is the nice part.

00:02:29.458 --> 00:02:33.788
You get the House of Yes room without it turning into a two a.m.

00:02:33.788 --> 00:02:34.194
night.

00:02:34.194 --> 00:02:37.103
Good one to bring someone who's never been.

00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:39.472
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:39.472 --> 00:02:45.493
At the show tonight, pick one instrument in the band and follow only it for a whole song.

00:02:45.493 --> 00:02:49.214
The bass, the trumpet, a hand drum, whatever grabs you.

00:02:49.214 --> 00:02:53.342
When your attention drifts back to the singer, bring it back.

00:02:53.342 --> 00:02:57.469
Then on the next song, let go and hear the whole thing again.

00:02:57.469 --> 00:03:03.084
It's like isolating one light in a setup, and it changes how the whole song sounds.

00:03:03.084 --> 00:03:06.941
Okay, something new today, and it's one from your corner.

00:03:06.941 --> 00:03:07.482
How A.I.

00:03:07.482 --> 00:03:10.256
image generators actually make a picture.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:13.504
Most of them are what's called diffusion models.

00:03:13.504 --> 00:03:15.534
The training starts backwards.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:20.202
You take a real photo and add a little random noise, like film grain.

00:03:20.202 --> 00:03:21.488
Then a little more.

00:03:21.488 --> 00:03:25.006
Keep going, and after enough steps it's pure static.

00:03:25.006 --> 00:03:25.953
No photo left.

00:03:25.953 --> 00:03:29.268
The model's whole job is to learn one small move.

00:03:29.268 --> 00:03:35.155
Look at a noisy image and guess what noise was added, so you can take a bit of it away.

00:03:35.155 --> 00:03:35.831
That's it.

00:03:35.831 --> 00:03:40.229
It practices that on millions of images, at every level of noise.

00:03:40.229 --> 00:03:43.612
Making a new picture runs it forward from nothing.

00:03:43.612 --> 00:03:45.168
Start with pure static.

00:03:45.168 --> 00:03:47.807
Ask the model to remove a little noise.

00:03:47.807 --> 00:03:49.092
Then a little more.

00:03:49.092 --> 00:03:55.114
Over a few dozen steps, shapes show up, then edges, then texture, until there's an image.

00:03:55.114 --> 00:03:57.008
The prompt steers each step.

00:03:57.008 --> 00:04:02.286
At every pass, the model leans the cleanup toward whatever matches your words.

00:04:02.286 --> 00:04:07.157
That's also why the same prompt gives you a different picture each time.

00:04:07.157 --> 00:04:09.187
The starting static is random.

00:04:09.187 --> 00:04:19.674
A different patch of noise grows into a different image, a bit like how the same scene photographed through different grain would never come out identical.

00:04:19.674 --> 00:04:24.748
And it explains the trouble with keeping a product consistent across edits.

00:04:24.748 --> 00:04:30.905
Each image is grown fresh from noise, so nothing forces the same logo to come back exactly.

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:36.250
Tools that do it well have to feed the model a reference picture at every step.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:42.813
So: calendar says gym at seven, Godinger till one, dinner at five-fifteen, and the show at seven.

00:04:42.813 --> 00:04:44.302
Follow one instrument.

00:04:44.302 --> 00:04:49.579
The answer: make a fresh random link, move things there, and kill the old one.

00:04:49.579 --> 00:04:51.000
Enjoy the show, Ziad.
